Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 03/16/2023]
1.  Direct capture calculated using experimental C2S values from Endt (1977), Vernotte et al. (1990), Vernotte et al. (1994), and Harrouz et al. (2022).
2.  Resonance energies are obtained from excitation energies and nuclear Q values.
3.  For all directly measured resonances, strengths are normalized to Er=603 keV value recommended in Paine & Sargood.
4.  Er=602 keV: from Paine & Sargood.
5.  Er=442 keV: assumed factor 2 uncertainty in proton width, since C2S=0.02 for J=5/2.
6.  Er=396 keV: assumed factor 3 uncertainty in proton width, since C2S=0.001 for J=5/2.
7.  Er=147 keV: we disregard the presumed level at "151 keV", see the detailed reasoning in Gribble et al. (2025); since the level is only weakly populated in
    Harrouz et al. (2022), we assume a factor 10 uncertainty for the proton width.
8.  Er=51 keV: ell=2 from Gribble et al. (2025).
9.  Er=171 keV: ell=3 and 5 are possible; deuteron angular distribution in Harrouz et al. looks poor, thus treat Gp for ell=3 as an upper limit.
10. Dimensionless single particle width estimated using r0=1.25 fm and a-0.65 fm, i.e., same optical model bound state parameters used as for extraction
    of experimental C2S values in Harrouz et al. 2022.
11. All directly measured resonance strengths are correlated because they are normalized to the strength of the 603 keV resonance (from Paine & Sargood,
    NPA 331, 389 (1979)). 
12. Contribution of -154 keV subthreshold resonance is negligible compared to DC component.
13. Additional information on some excitation energies and the unambiguous 3/2+ assignment for Er=18.7 keV is adopted from Gribble et al. (2024).
